# The accessibility conformance scene. Every assertion here is read from
# the PLATFORM'S OWN accessibility tree — the one an assistive client
# walks — never from kaya's scene model. Reading the model would make
# these steps agree with themselves and prove nothing; the claim under
# test is that the native control publishes a correct accessibility
# surface without kaya synthesizing one.
#
# EVERY WIDGET KIND is asserted, because the two props are universal:
# thirteen working kinds and one forgotten is exactly the shape that
# ships (tools/check-universal-props.sh greps for the lowering; this is
# where the runtime agrees or does not).
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the
# scene-ready wait (check-steps holds that line for every scene).

# DERIVED names: identified but deliberately unlabelled, so what the
# tree reports is whatever the control took from its own caption. This
# is the free-by-construction half of the wrap-native bet, and it is the
# half no framework that draws its own widgets gets for nothing. A
# label's caption is its content, so it derives too.
expect_ax button#0 "button/Save"
expect_ax checkbox#0 "checkbox/Details"
expect_ax button#1 "button/Reset"
expect_ax label#0 "label/Ready"

# AUTHORED names: a bare text field, an editor, a slider, a progress bar
# and an image draw nothing an assistive client can read, which is
# exactly where an app must supply the name.
expect_ax entry#0 "field/Full name"
expect_ax textarea#0 "field/Notes"
expect_ax slider#0 "slider/Volume"
expect_ax progress#0 "progress/Loading"
expect_ax image#0 "image/Logo"

# HINTS: what ACTIVATING a control does — the one accessibility prop
# that is not universal, because it needs an activation to describe.
# Apple speaks it as written and Android prefixes "double tap to", so
# the authored text is a verb phrase that reads correctly after both.
# The root admits it on activation kinds only; a hint on a label or an
# image dies there rather than reaching four backends and not the fifth.
expect_ax_hint button#0 "save the draft"
expect_ax_hint checkbox#0 "show more detail"

# The CHOICE kinds. A chooser is a chooser on every platform and spelled
# differently on each (AXPopUpButton, a dropdown role on Compose,
# ComboBox on AT-SPI and UIA) — `combobox` is the closed set's one name
# for it. The inline presentation of the same contract is a group of
# choices, and `group` is what every platform agrees it is.
expect_ax select#0 "combobox/Color"
expect_ax radio#0 "group/Size"

# CONTAINERS are GROUPS to an assistive client — the reason these props
# are universal rather than kind-scoped — and naming one is how an app
# declares it a group. All four container kinds, because "a container is
# a group" is a claim about containers, not about rows. Every backend
# needs a different step to make it true (see the guest's note); the
# observable is identical everywhere.
expect_ax column#0 "group/Form"
expect_ax grid#0 "group/Cells"
expect_ax scroll#0 "group/Feed"
expect_ax row#0 "group/Actions"

# A named group is a landmark in the tree, not a lid over it: its
# children stay individually reachable. That is the other half of the
# container claim, and the half that regressed once already.
expect_ax button#2 "button/Cancel"
expect_ax button#3 "button/OK"
